Yes. There is a real distinction between studying reality from outside it and becoming part of the apparatus yourself.

Some of science’s most consequential knowledge has come from people who were not merely observers, but participants, operators, patients, explorers, test subjects, or self-experimenters. In those cases the person is simultaneously sensor, actuator, recorder, and affected system. That produces forms of knowledge that are difficult to obtain secondhand: pain, uncertainty, fear, adaptation, perceptual distortion, bodily limits, unexpected side effects, tacit technique.

The danger is when that gets romanticized into sacrifice for its own sake. Suffering is not inherently informative. A useful distinction is:

\text{experience} \neq \text{experiment}

An experience becomes scientifically valuable when it is captured, contextualized, compared, and made reconstructable.

So the preservation problem matters. A good record would not just say what happened. It would preserve several layers:

* State before — baseline, expectations, assumptions.
* Intervention — what was actually done.
* Immediate phenomenology — what the person perceived and felt.
* Objective measurements — whatever could be independently recorded.
* Interpretation at the time — what they believed was happening.
* Later reinterpretation — what hindsight changed.
* Costs and harms — including things the participant would not repeat.
* Transferable lesson — what general principle survived the specific event.
* Uncertainty — where memory, causality, or interpretation remain unclear.

That separation is important because memory is powerful but reconstructive. The vividness of an experience can make its interpretation feel equally certain when those are actually different things.

You could almost store these as experiential scientific objects:

E=
(\text{conditions},
\text{action},
\text{observation},
\text{felt experience},
\text{consequence},
\text{interpretation},
\text{later delta})

Then compare many such objects across people and contexts.

That preserves something conventional scientific papers often compress away: the actor-level texture of discovery.

And it gives you a second-order layer:

What did interacting with the phenomenon do to the observer?

Because sometimes the instrument changes while measuring the world.

That is especially important with humans. A person can enter an experiment believing one thing, endure something, learn from it, become altered by it, and then perceive subsequent events differently. The observer at t_1 is no longer identical to the observer at t_0.

So the real record is not merely:

\text{world before} \rightarrow \text{world after}

but

(\text{world},\text{observer})_{t_0}
\rightarrow
\text{interaction}
\rightarrow
(\text{world},\text{observer})_{t_1}

That is a richer scientific object.

And for preservation, I would keep the raw account and the later abstraction together, rather than replacing one with the other. The raw memory preserves the grain; the abstraction preserves the transferable operator.

That fits your broader reconstruction instinct well: preserve enough provenance that later readers can recover not only the conclusion, but how reality pushed back against the person who learned it.